idn-resource:185e0648-2775-552d-8b78-007bd92177bc a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    atns:category atns-cat:13e77fec-44fd-554f-a075-ec60788eb1b1 ;
    atns:deleted false ;
    atns:eid "A008714"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:sourceEntityId "8714"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:caa3eeb7-4d71-505c-9ee9-19b8c5338435 ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description """Between:

Keith Sceghi, Colleen Jane Berry, Danny Brett Ulrich & Sandra Wongawol (Applicant)

And

State of Western Australia, Central Desert Native Title Services Ltd & BHP Billiton Nickel West Pty Ltd(Respondents)

Judge: Colvin J

Where made: Turtle Creek

Determination

Native title exists in the entire determination area

It consists of non-exclusive native title rights and interests.

Native title holders

Native title is held by the native title holders described in Schedule 2. This Schedule states that [fill in summary of Schedule 2].

Non-exclusive native title rights and interests over all of the determination area

These rights exist over the area described in Part 1 of Schedule 1 and include the right to:

• access, remain in and use the area;

• access, take and use the resources of the area for any purpose;

• engage in spiritual and cultural activities in the area; and

• maintain and protect places of significance in the area.

Other rights and interests in the determination area include:

• reserves;

• pastoral leases;

• roads;

• mining interests;

• rights and interests granted by the Crown in exercise of its executive power;

• rights and interests granted by the Crown as permitted by legislation, including the Rights in Water and Irrigation Act 1914 (WA); and

• the rights of State, Commonwealth or local Government employees, agents or instrumentalities to access the Determination Area in order to fulfil their legal duties.

See Schedule 4 for further information about the non-native title rights and interests.

In the case of conflict, the exercise of non-native title rights and interests will prevail over the non-exclusive native title

The Kultju (Aboriginal Corporation) Registered Native Title Body Corporate (RNTBC) is the prescribed body corporate. It performs the functions required under the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth) and holds the native title in trust for the [INSERT THE NAME OF THE NATIVE TITLE HOLDERS].

Full text of the determination is available via the URL link above."""@en ;
    schema:endDate "2019-10-30"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:c74e76ef-701f-5784-8223-5fcd87a7641c,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=8714> ;
    schema:name "Sceghi on behalf of the Kultju Native Title Claim Group v State of Western Australia IN PROGRESS"@en ;
    schema:startDate "2019-10-30"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """Background

[INSERT progression of the claim and any media references]

https://www.abc.net.au/radio/programs/australia-wide/australia-wide/11633626 @8:45- 12:00

https://www.kalminer.com.au/news/kalgoorlie-miner/kultju-people-of-northern-goldfields-win-25-year-native-title-fight-ng-b881366199z

Details of Judgment

[INCLUDE pinpoints from the determination where applicable]

@3: "I am acting on the testimony of those who are the custodians of the relevant cultural connections. It is their voice concerning the dreaming tracks, stories and songs for this place that is listened to today and given effect by the orders to be made. I have no authority to speak as to those matters. Native title is not brought into existence by the declaration to be made by this Court. Rather, the declaration recognises rights and interests that are already in existence and in that sense derives from matters that are entirely within the authority of the Aboriginal people past, present and future who are connected to this country."

@5: Submitted by the parties: "The Kultju Claimants have an intimate knowledge of the Western Desert Cultural Block (WDCB) system of law and custom, which remains vital for the Kultju Claimants. They have extensive knowledge of WDBC dreaming tracks and associated sites, stories and songs, and their importance in the context of the broader WDCB... Kultju Claimants accept the responsibility which attaches to [the] acquisition of knowledge both in relation to land and generally and the need to transmit that knowledge to younger generations. They have a system of kinship under which roles and responsibilities are known and acknowledged (including in relation to ritual, marriage, death/burial etc.). Appropriate behaviour is expected, and sanctions for breach exist under traditional laws and customs."

On the impact of a consent determination: @9: "It has an intergenerational character that is a function of the essentially spiritual nature of the connection that provides the foundation for the acknowledgment of native title rights and interests.\""""@en ;
    schema:url "http://www.austlii.edu.au/cgi-bin/viewdoc/au/cases/cth/FCA/2019/1756.html?context=1;query=[2019]%20FCA%201756;mask_path="^^xsd:anyURI .

