idn-resource:2adcd946-3a21-5098-b5e6-369fe88e2af4 a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    atns:category atns-cat:13e77fec-44fd-554f-a075-ec60788eb1b1 ;
    atns:deleted false ;
    atns:eid "A003941"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:sourceEntityId "3941"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:1682bda6-86ad-57ab-b605-73fb7027939d ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description """The Geraldton ‘Bayalgu Kicking Goals Higher’ Shared Responsibility Agreement (SRA) was developed between the Geraldton Indigenous Community, the Commonwealth Department of Families, Community Services and Indigenous Affairs (FaCSIA), Oxiana Golden Grove Pty Ltd, Central Earthmoving, Mid West Football Academy Indigenous Parent Body and the Clontarf Foundation: Mid West Football Academy on 29 May 2007. The SRA aims to aid young people in making a more successful transition from school to the workplace.

Shared Responsibility Agreements are agreements between governments and Indigenous communities to provide discretionary funding in return for community obligations. The new arrangements developed from an initiative of the Council of Australian Governments and replace the previous ATSIC system of funding. For more information see ‘Shared Responsibility Agreement’ below."""@en ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:5996de0c-8cc6-5f14-8901-d839af36a4d4,
        atns-sub:6a8815da-e29d-506f-85fd-c64c8807fea2,
        atns-sub:9073e633-80a9-5ba7-8892-f556bd0a0612,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=3941> ;
    schema:name "Geraldton 'Bayalgu Kicking Goals Higher' Shared Responsibility Agreement (SRA)"@en ;
    schema:startDate "2007-05-28"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """Purpose

The purpose of this SRA is to aid the Mid West Football Association (MWFA) in their work helping young indigenous men make a successful transition from school to the workforce (see generally Clontarf Foundation 2006). This SRA will focus on direct intervention, by providing the men with the social and educational support to either continue with their education or to engage with the broader Geraldton economy. The vehicle for this intervention is the Bayalgu Training Program, which consists of:
(a) Occupational health and safety/emergency procedures from Central Earthmoving;
(b) Mining safety awareness course;
(c) Bobcat, forklift and small engine training;
(d) Traffic management course;
(e) First aid training; and
(f) Work experience at Central Earthmoving and Oxiana Golden Grove.

Shared Responsibilities

The Geraldton Indigenous Community will form the Midwest Football Academy Indigenous Parent Body to work with Geraldton Secondary College to increase attendance and retention rates, and will be responsible for the operation and maintenance of the MWFA bus.

Families and individuals will participate in the Bayalgu training program; ensure that the MWFA programs have an 85% attendance rate and the Bayalgu program a 100% rate; undertake volunteer work at MWFA training sessions; and attend requested parent teacher interviews at the Geraldton Secondary College.

Performance Indicators

The SRA provides for the following performance indicators:
(1) The number of students attaining year 10 and 12 standards, to be provided once by the MWFA;
(2) The number of people competed accredited training, to be provided on a six-monthly basis by the MWFA; and
(3) The number of people employed after training, to be provided on a six-monthly basis by MWFA, Oxiana Golden Grove Pty Ltd and Central Earthmoving."""@en .

idn-resource:2adf6dc5-f27d-55ba-ac52-35b9f51dc442 a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    atns:category atns-cat:13e77fec-44fd-554f-a075-ec60788eb1b1 ;
    atns:deleted false ;
    atns:eid "A007492"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:sourceEntityId "7492"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:09f0a068-ce65-5c11-8dec-f081d1a81ec2 ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description """The Pormpuraaw Township Community Development Indigenous Land Use Agreement (ILUA) was signed on 29 February 2016 between:

• the State of Queensland;

• Michael Ross and others on their own behalf and behalf of the Cape York United #1 Claim Group;

• Doris William and others on their own behalf and on behalf of the Traditional Owners and as agents for the Cape York Party; and

• Pormpuraaw Aboriginal Shire Council.

The purpose of the ILUA is to provide consent for various acts in relation to the construction and leasing of social housing."""@en ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:c74e76ef-701f-5784-8223-5fcd87a7641c,
        atns-sub:ffe61b3b-8b29-5264-89ac-758a03928ea5,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=7492> ;
    schema:name "Pormpuraaw Township Community Development Indigenous Land Use Agreement (ILUA)"@en ;
    schema:spatialCoverage <https://data.idnau.org/pid/nntt/QI2016-004> ;
    schema:startDate "2016-08-29"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """Details of the Agreement:

The agreement area covers about 8.5 sq km in the vicinity of Pormpuraaw in the Gulf of Carpentaria. For details of the agreement area see Schedule 1 to the agreement attached below.

Commencement:

The ILUA commenced on 29 February 2016 (the date that it was signed by the last of the parties). It was entered onto the Register of Indigenous Land Use Agreements on 29 August 2016.

Native Title Provisions:

The parties consent to acts that include:

• the grant of homeownership lease;

• the grant of a social housing lease;

• the construction, operation, use, maintenance, or repair of social housing;

• the grant of residential tenancy agreements to the tenants of social housing

• the Council doing acts in relation to infrastructure and operations;

• the grant of council housing tenure;

• the grant of a development and residential tenures and the doing of certain developments;

• the grant of State lease;

• the creation, use, and management of the New Reserves;

• the revoking of a State Reserve (whole or in part), the surrender of the DOGIT and the redescription of the Deed of Grant in Trust (DOGIT) to include the revoked State Reserve area;

• the change of trusteeship of a State Reserve or a Council Reserve;

• the change of a State Reserve's purpose to "the provision of services beneficial to Aboriginal people particularly concerned with land"."""@en ;
    schema:url "http://www.nntt.gov.au/searchRegApps/NativeTitleRegisters/Pages/ILUA_details.aspx?NNTT_Fileno=QI2016/004"^^xsd:anyURI .

idn-resource:2ae4c797-6304-589a-b6e8-e5c3f9528911 a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    atns:category atns-cat:13e77fec-44fd-554f-a075-ec60788eb1b1 ;
    atns:deleted false ;
    atns:eid "A008216"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:sourceEntityId "8216"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:09f0a068-ce65-5c11-8dec-f081d1a81ec2 ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description """The Mt. Augustus and Combined Thiin-Mah, Warriyangka, Tharrkari, Jiwarli Indigenous Land Use Agreement (ILUA) was agreed, under the provisions of the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth), between Mt. Augustus Station (1980) Pty Ltd and Woodgoomungooh Aboriginal Corporation.

The purpose of the ILUA is to gain consent from the Thiin-Mah, Warriyangka, Tharrkari, Jiwarli (TMWTJ) peoples for the renewal of pastoral leases, the granting of pastoral leases over stock routes and reserves, and the licensing of low impact tourism and agricultural activities on the land.

The Native Title Representative Body for this area is the Yamatji Marlpa Aboriginal Corporation."""@en ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:9c404ff9-19e6-5e64-a8bd-2e3b39773146,
        atns-sub:bda82010-54a4-5bac-8bf5-4b5341822974,
        atns-sub:c74e76ef-701f-5784-8223-5fcd87a7641c,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=8216> ;
    schema:name "Mt. Augustus and Combined Thiin-Mah, Warriyangka, Tharrkari, Jiwarli Indigenous Land Use Agreement (ILUA)"@en ;
    schema:spatialCoverage <https://data.idnau.org/pid/nntt/WI2020-005> ;
    schema:startDate "2020-06-15"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """Details of the Agreement:

Agreement Area:

The extract from the Register of Indigenous Land Use Agreements describes the area covered by this ILUA as the part of Mt Augustus Pastoral Lease that falls within Native Title Determination Application WAD464/2016 Combined Thiin-Mah, Warriyangka, Tharrkari, Jiwarli (WC2016/003). The ILUA area covers approx. 218 sq km and is located approx. 114 km southwest of Paraburdoo. For more information, see Schedule B and the map attached below.

Commencement and Termination:

The ILUA commenced when all parties become signatories. It operates as an ILUA, and in relation to the agreed acts, from the date of its registration. This agreement will be terminated when the parties mutually agree in writing, or when the Pastoral Lease comes to an end.

Native Title Provisions:

Right to negotiate:

The parties to the ILUA agreed that the right to negotiate provisions of the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth) do not apply to the future acts agreed to under this ILUA.

Extinguishment:

The parties agree that the non-extinguishment principle applies to the granting of Pastoral lease and other tenures over the Stock Routes and Reserves. As per s 24EB(3) of the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth), any of the activities done under the pastoral lease which may be inconsistent with native title rights and interests, do not extinguish them.

Future act provisions:

The TMWTJ People and the TMWTJ PBC agree to the doing of the following future acts:

• the extension, renewal, re-making or replacement of the Pastoral Lease from time to time;

• the grant of a license, permit, or authorisation to conduct activities for agricultural purposes (including, the diversification and intensification of pastoral related activities) on the Pastoral Lease;

• the grant of leases, licenses, and permits to conduct Low Impact Tourism activities in the Pastoral Lease area; and

• the grant of pastoral leases in relation to all or part of the Stock Routes or Reserves (excluding reserve 26808, Barlee Range Nature Reserve).

Native Title in the Agreement Area:

The ILUA is within the determination area of Eagles on behalf of the Combined Thiin-Mah, Warriyangka, Tharrkari and Jiwarli People v State of Western Australia [2019] FCA 508 (FCA file no.: WAD464/2016; NNTT file no.: WCD2019/003)."""@en ;
    schema:url "http://www.nntt.gov.au/searchRegApps/NativeTitleRegisters/Pages/ILUA_details.aspx?NNTT_Fileno=WI2020/005"^^xsd:anyURI .

