idn-resource:5324c7bd-0d74-53e5-bd75-22ef02fdf178 a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    atns:category atns-cat:13e77fec-44fd-554f-a075-ec60788eb1b1 ;
    atns:deleted false ;
    atns:eid "A003042"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:sourceEntityId "3418"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:51e799aa-68a2-5df5-b3b6-ce3837c24612 ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description """The Withdrawal – Infrastructure Agreement is a template Mediation Withdrawal Agreement provided by the National Native Title Tribunal (NNTT).

It is envisaged by the NNTT that these agreements will be used to assist Indigenous Australians and non-Indigenous Australians to negotiate Mediation Withdrawal Agreement that can be tailored to their specific needs and circumstances.

The Withdrawal – Infrastructure Agreement is designed to be made between a registered native title party and a company that is a respondent party to a native title claim. The agreement would see the company withdraw from the native title claim in return for certain commitments from the native title party. These commitments would be that, notwithstanding the eventual outcome of the native title claim, the native title party would agree that they will not prejudice the company’s rights and interest in the claimed area, whether by political, legal or other means.

The template agreement also sets out other matters, including confidentiality requirements."""@en ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:746b3b59-88ea-5685-81f7-1558875d7c47,
        atns-sub:9e5e8f61-69be-5e2a-a9cd-58fb000349bd,
        atns-sub:c74e76ef-701f-5784-8223-5fcd87a7641c,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=3418> ;
    schema:name "Withdrawal – Infrastructure Agreement"@en ;
    schema:text """The NNTT has issued template agreements covering six categories, being:
1. Future Act Agreements
2. Intra-Aboriginal Agreements
3. Mediation Withdrawal Agreements
4. Negotiating Framework Agreements
5. Pastoral Agreements
6. Reconciliation Agreements Principals.

Please see the ‘Template Agreement’ link at the bottom of this page for further information.

Please see the National Native Title Tribunal's DISCLAIMER in relation to these agreements."""@en ;
    schema:url "http://www.nntt.gov.au/agreements/files/TemplateWithdrawalInfrastructureMWA/"^^xsd:anyURI .

idn-resource:5326774d-e490-56db-b52d-3ba591329472 a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    atns:category atns-cat:13e77fec-44fd-554f-a075-ec60788eb1b1 ;
    atns:deleted false ;
    atns:eid "A007282"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:sourceEntityId "7282"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:09f0a068-ce65-5c11-8dec-f081d1a81ec2 ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description """'The Wulli Wulli Claim was filed on 17 July 2000. It covers an area of approximately 5,432 square kilometres and includes the towns of Theodore and Cracow, pastoral properties, Precipice & Isla Gorge National Parks, state forests and sections of the Wulli Wulli People’s beloved Dawson River.' (Banana Shire Council,2015) History shows Wulli Wulli people lived here up to 10, 000 years ago when they used the land to hunt and gather, and used weapons, traditional customs and spiritual beliefs.' (Central Telegraph,2015)

The Wulli Wulli People/Onkaparinga Indigenous Land Use Agreement (ILUA) was agreed between the Wulli Wulli People and the Wondekai on 29 January 2016. The purpose of the ILUA is to provide consent for a range of acts, whether or not they are ‘future acts’, regarding the access to and use of the land subject to pastoral leases. The agreement area is located within the external boundary of the Wulli Wulli People native title claim."""@en ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:9335b2f1-4915-55cc-9736-2ded7c4a2008,
        atns-sub:bda82010-54a4-5bac-8bf5-4b5341822974,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=7282> ;
    schema:name "Wulli Wulli People/Onkaparinga Indigenous Land Use Agreement (ILUA)"@en ;
    schema:spatialCoverage <https://data.idnau.org/pid/nntt/QI2015-071> ;
    schema:startDate "2016-01-29"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """Details of Agreement: The ILUA is located in the Banana Shire Council. The agreement covers about 203 sq km, located about 18 km south west of Cracow.

Commencement: This ILUA commenced on 29 January 2016.

Native Title Provisions: The ILUA does not have statements that reference subsection 24EB(1) or 24EBA(1) or (4) of the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth).

Background to the Agreement: The ILUA is an area agreement which allows access to the land subject to pastoral leases.

Native Title in the ILUA Area: The ILUA is in the native title claim area which includes the land and waters the subject of Federal Court Proceedings QUD 6006 of 2000. It is one of six agreements which were subject to a conditional determination by the Federal Court of Australia on 13 August 2015 (QUD6006/2000) and later subject to a consent determination by the Federal Court of Australia on 18 July 2017."""@en ;
    schema:url "http://www.nntt.gov.au/searchRegApps/NativeTitleRegisters/Pages/ILUA_details.aspx?NNTT_Fileno=QI2015/071"^^xsd:anyURI .

idn-resource:5328c6de-45b9-55ab-a3dc-33589c7b8160 a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:references idn-resource:30e07df4-be4f-5bac-899f-28315e2b7615,
        idn-resource:3e21beee-d7ef-5e76-a3fd-43c0d7b04eb1,
        idn-resource:c308f710-4dc3-548a-b757-b5016ca20cf6 ;
    atns:category atns-cat:13e77fec-44fd-554f-a075-ec60788eb1b1 ;
    atns:country atns-country:244f6961-729f-5b21-8fc9-baff9dd9af7e ;
    atns:deleted false ;
    atns:eid "A002518"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:sourceEntityId "2918"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:1682bda6-86ad-57ab-b605-73fb7027939d ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description """The Yalata 'New Scout Troop for Kids' Shared Responsibility Agreement (SRA) is one of two SRAs signed in Yalata in May 2005. The SRAs hope to increase the confidence of local children and improve community safety.

The 'New Scout Troop for kids' SRA was signed between the Yalata community, Commonwealth and State governments, the local police and local school.

Shared Responsibility Agreements are agreements between governments and Indigenous communities to provide discretionary funding in return for community obligations. The new arrangements developed from an initiative of the Council of Australian Governments and replace the previous ATSIC system of funding. For more information see ‘Shared Responsibility Agreement’ below."""@en ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:5996de0c-8cc6-5f14-8901-d839af36a4d4,
        atns-sub:6a8815da-e29d-506f-85fd-c64c8807fea2,
        atns-sub:ffe61b3b-8b29-5264-89ac-758a03928ea5,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:location "Yalata, South Australia, Australia"@en ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=2918> ;
    schema:name "Yalata 'New Scout Troop for Kids' Shared Responsibility Agreement (SRA)"@en ;
    schema:startDate "2005-01-01"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """Purpose

The SRA aims to increase the confidence and leadership skills of children in Yalata. The parties to the SRA will work to set up a scout troop to promote these activities. Yalata's school also needs upgrading, and the SRA provides arrangements to assist with refurbishment and security.

Shared Responsibilities

The Commonwealth government is contributing $114,000 to this SRA. These funds will provide scout uniforms, equipment and travel. Along with the South Australian government, the Commonwealth will fund the painting of the school and the provision of new equipment, including shade areas. The South Australian government will fund scout leader's salaries.

The SRA works in conjunction with Community Development Employment Projects (CDEP). CDEP participants will be trained in mentoring and leadership, and become scout leaders. CDEP workers will also assist with the school upgrade.

The community at Yalata will run scout meetings and camping activities as part of the scout program. A community artist will assist the school upgrade by helping children to paint murals.

A 'no school, no scouts' policy will apply to the running of the scout troop. Children who attend schoool for 85 percent of the year will be permitted to attend the National Scout Jamboree.

The Yalata SRAs are administered by the Ceduna Indigenous Coordination Centre."""@en ;
    schema:url "http://www.indigenous.gov.au/sra/sa/fact_sheets/sa04.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI .

