idn-resource:6db78398-3444-5ebb-b4d2-19f6a6729038 a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:references idn-resource:7b928a0d-47c5-537a-9136-066ae7f542f4 ;
    atns:category atns-cat:13e77fec-44fd-554f-a075-ec60788eb1b1 ;
    atns:deleted false ;
    atns:eid "A004193"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:sourceEntityId "4193"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:09f0a068-ce65-5c11-8dec-f081d1a81ec2 ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description """The Eastern Kuku Yalanji & Ergon Indigenous Land Use Agreement (ILUA) was agreed between Ergon Energy (APPLICANT); and Agnes Walker, Eileen Walker, Hazel Douglas, John Walker Jnr., Peter Fischer on 5 October 2007.

The purpose of the ILUA is to provide for the relationship between native title rights and interests and the interests of Ergon Energy.

The ILUA extract does not state a specific period for its operation."""@en ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:17d993e0-d19e-5349-a48e-4de57b1dba59,
        atns-sub:81edd075-cb8e-5a7e-a65d-a47cec815898,
        atns-sub:9335b2f1-4915-55cc-9736-2ded7c4a2008,
        atns-sub:c74e76ef-701f-5784-8223-5fcd87a7641c,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=4193> ;
    schema:name "Eastern Kuku Yalanji & Ergon Indigenous Land Use Agreement (ILUA)"@en ;
    schema:startDate "2007-10-05"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """The parties to the ILUA consent to the doing of all acts necessary for the maintenance of Ergon Energy infrastructure. The ILUA states that any acts will be subject to an agreed cultural heritage management plan.

This ILUA includes agreement that 'future acts' may be done. The parties also agree that the right to negotiate provisions of the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth) do not apply to future acts, as the alternative consultation provisions are to be followed instead. Under this Act any activity, such as a grant of land, that may affect native title rights is defined as a ‘future act’ and must comply with the future act provisions of the Act in order to be valid.

This ILUA is one of 15 resulting from the Eastern Kuku Yalanji 1994 native title claim over some 144,000 hectares, which was mainly timber reserve and unallocated Crown land. In 1997 the Eastern Kuku Yalanji proposed a settlement with the Queensland Government, which was agreed to in principle by most parties in 2005. (Queensland Government)

Approximately 250,000 hectares of land were given back to the Eastern Kuku Yalanji people with 64,000 hectares designated as freehold land to be used for conservation with 16,500 of this proportion to be set aside for use as residential or economic development. The majority of the land will be co-managed by the Eastern Kuku Yalanji people and the Queensland Parks & Wildlife Service (QPWS) (The Australian). At the sign-off in Wujal Wujal in April 2007, then premier Peter Beattie congratulated all involved in the process, saying that it was ‘the most significant ILUA [sic] ever struck in Queensland.’ (Kantri Leif)"""@en .

