    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description "This Indigenous Land Use Agreement (ILUA) was agreed between Santos GLNG Pty Ltd, Petronas Australia Pty Limited and Louis Boyd Toby and others on their own behalf and on behalf of the Gangulu Native Title Claim Group. The purpose of this ILUA is to provide consent for a range of acts by Santos and Petronas in relation to a 435 kilometre gas transmission pipeline that forms part of their joint venture Gladstone Liquefied Natural Gas (GLNG) project."@en ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:29221ef9-81d0-59aa-a9c9-744b71ff8349,
        atns-sub:81edd075-cb8e-5a7e-a65d-a47cec815898,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=5212> ;
    schema:name "Santos Petronas Gangulu GLNG Indigenous Land Use Agreement (ILUA)"@en ;
    schema:startDate "2010-04-07"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """The GLNG project that this ILUA is intended to facilitate consists of the construction and operation of a 435 kilometre gas transmission pipeline between upstream coal seam gas fields in central Queensland, and the establishment of a proposed natural gas liquefaction and export facility on Curtis Island near Gladstone. The project also involves the establishment of all other infrastructure necessary for either the pipeline or the gas facility.

Pursuant to this ILUA, the registered Native Title Applicants (being Louis Boyd Toby and others on their own behalf and on behalf of the Gangulu Native Title Claim Group) give their consent to the grant of all approvals and the undertaking of a number of activities in relation to the Santos-Petronas GLNG project - whether or not they constitute future acts. More specifically, the activities to which consent has been given include the following:

• Access and entry to the pipeline land;

• The construction, operation, use, maintenance, repair, further development, decommissioning and rehabilitation of the pipeline on the pipeline land; and

• Any other activities to be conducted within the ILUA area pursuant to the Pipeline License or any other project-related approval.

In terms of the relevant approvals, the Native Title Applicants grant Santos GLNG the permission of the Gangulu Native Title Claim Group to enter the pipeline land for the purposes of constructing and operating the pipeline, as required by ss 401(2) and 399(1)(b)(ii) of the Petroleum and Gas (Production and Safety) Act 2004 (Qld). If permission is also applied for under Part 5 of this Act, then the parties agree that such permission will also be granted.

Right to negotiate

The parties agree that the right to negotiate in Subdivision P, Division 3, Part 2 of the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth) will not apply to any of the future acts to which consent has been granted under this ILUA.

Non-extinguishment principle

The parties also agree that the non-extinguishment principle in s 238 of the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth) will apply to the future acts to which consent has been granted under this ILUA. This means that acts that would ordinarily result in the extinguishment of native title will not have this effect. Instead, any native title rights and interests will merely be suspended for the duration of these acts.

Background to this ILUA

The ILUA area is subject to the Gangulu People's registered native title claim, which was filed with the Federal Court of Australia as proceeding QUD 6144 of 1998. This claim related to approximately 11.956 square kilometres of land and waters in central Queensland. It was dismissed on 3 June 2009.

This ILUA is one of 42 agreements entered into by Santos Ltd and Petronas Australia Pty Limited with Aboriginal peoples for the purposes of their joint venture, the GLNG project. These agreements form the largest body of agreements to be signed with Aboriginal groups in the history of Australia's resources industry. According to a media release by Santos GLNG Pty Ltd, these agreements will lead to new employment, training and enterprise opportunities for the Aboriginal communities involved."""@en ;
    schema:url "http://www.nntt.gov.au/Indigenous-Land-Use-Agreements/Search-Registered-ILUAs/ILUA%20Register/2009/QI2009.032/ILUARegisterExport.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI .

idn-resource:7842327e-8938-500c-8cdf-a52f2e16eb4c a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:references idn-resource:93a55b1d-47bd-59e4-ac71-306416103c5e ;
    atns:category atns-cat:13e77fec-44fd-554f-a075-ec60788eb1b1 ;
    atns:country atns-country:244f6961-729f-5b21-8fc9-baff9dd9af7e ;
    atns:deleted false ;
    atns:eid "A002160"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:sourceEntityId "2553"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:1682bda6-86ad-57ab-b605-73fb7027939d ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description """The Lockhart River Shared Responsibility Agreement (SRA) was signed on 3 December 2003. This SRA is part of the Cape York Council of Australian Governments (COAG) trial.

The SRA signed in 2003 was followed by a Community Plan in 2004. The focus of this SRA is community planning."""@en ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:5996de0c-8cc6-5f14-8901-d839af36a4d4,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:location "Cape York, Queensland, Australia"@en ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=2553> ;
    schema:name "Lockhart River 'Empower the Community' Shared Responsibility Agreement (SRA)"@en ;
    schema:startDate "2003-12-03"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """The agreement signed in 2003 committed the community to developing a community plan related to economic development and education. The Commonwealth and Queensland governments agreed to assist with the plan.

The Community Plan 2004-08 outlines the community's desired outcomes and performance indicators for issues including housing, justice, employment and men's and women's issues. Priorities identified in the plan include:
- develop outstations
- improve road and sea access
- break the unemployment cycle
- improve housing
- improve community facilities
- celebrate and teach traditional ways
- improve education provision.

The Queensland government has funded a community development officer to assist the community.

The Lockhart River community has developed a Puuya Strategy to guide relations with government.

The Lockhart River SRA is administered by the Cape York Indigenous Coordination Centre."""@en ;
    schema:url "http://www.indigenous.gov.au/sra/qld/fact_sheets/qld05.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI .

idn-resource:78448f3d-f110-52d3-9db6-adc117ea0728 a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    atns:category atns-cat:13e77fec-44fd-554f-a075-ec60788eb1b1 ;
    atns:country atns-country:244f6961-729f-5b21-8fc9-baff9dd9af7e ;
    atns:deleted false ;
    atns:eid "A002670"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:sourceEntityId "3060"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:1682bda6-86ad-57ab-b605-73fb7027939d ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description """The Indigenous community at Barkuma has signed a Shared Responsibility Agreement (SRA) with the Commonwealth Government to facilitate repairs for the Barkuma (Kurri Kurri) Neighbourhood Centre. The Centre is currently the location for services such as counselling, emergency accommodation and programs for youth and disabled people. The Centre also acts as a local meeting place, but is in need of repair and upgrades.

Shared Responsibility Agreements are agreements between governments and Indigenous communities to provide discretionary funding in return for community obligations. The new arrangements developed from an initiative of the Council of Australian Governments and replace the previous ATSIC system of funding. For more information see ‘Shared Responsibility Agreement’ below."""@en ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:5996de0c-8cc6-5f14-8901-d839af36a4d4,
        atns-sub:d15dd1de-4696-50c8-9f10-2e95eea88e6f,
        atns-sub:ffe61b3b-8b29-5264-89ac-758a03928ea5,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:location "Kurri Kurri, New South Wales, Australia"@en ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=3060> ;
    schema:name "Barkuma 'Better Facilities for the Neighbourhood Centre' Shared Responsibility Agreement (SRA)"@en ;
    schema:startDate "2005-05-11"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """Purpose

As outlined above, the Barkuma (Kurri Kurri) Neighbourhood Centre provides vital services to the local Indigenous Community. These services support an ethic of safety and strong local families. Under the SRA, the Centre will be refurbished, the surrounding fence repaired and the walkway upgraded.

Shared Responsibilities

The Commonwealth Government has committed $50,000 to fund the refurbishment. Following the upgrade, an administrative support position will be created to be filled by a Community Development Employment Projects (CDEP) participant.

The Community has agreed to fundraise to contribute to the cost of the centre. They will also develop more activities to help local families.

The Barkuma SRA is administered by the Coffs Harbour Indigenous Coordination Centre."""@en ;
    schema:url "https://apps.indigenous.gov.au/sra/search/DisplayDocuments.aspx?id=29&name=(Barkuma%20(Kurri%20Kurri)&title=Better%20facilities%20for%20the%20neighbourhood%20centre&signDate=11-May-2005"^^xsd:anyURI .

