idn-resource:9de5f7b7-7a89-568f-b161-0dbb7f749811 a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    atns:category atns-cat:13e77fec-44fd-554f-a075-ec60788eb1b1 ;
    atns:deleted false ;
    atns:eid "A007486"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:sourceEntityId "7486"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:09f0a068-ce65-5c11-8dec-f081d1a81ec2,
        atns-subcat:177cef4d-1963-57e3-9b5c-508480daf336 ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description """The agreement area covers approximately 908.5 sq metres and is located approximately 3.9 kms west of Valencia Creek next to the Avon River.

The Gunaikurnai Land and Waters Aboriginal Corporation, Peter D Howson and Thomas A Howson and State of Victoria Indigenous Land Use Agreement (ILUA) was agreed on 12 August 2016 under the provisions of the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth)between the following parties:

• Gunaikurnai Land and Waters Aboriginal Corporation;

• Peter D Howson and Thomas A Howson; and

• The State of Victoria.

The purpose of the ILUA is to provide consent for extinguishment of native title rights and interests in the agreement area.

The Native Title Representative Body for this area is Native Title Services Victoria Ltd."""@en ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:9c404ff9-19e6-5e64-a8bd-2e3b39773146,
        atns-sub:c74e76ef-701f-5784-8223-5fcd87a7641c,
        atns-sub:dbff1607-a2a3-5baf-995d-8c9bb0552665,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=7486> ;
    schema:name "Gunaikurnai Land and Waters Aboriginal Corporation, Peter D Howson and Thomas A Howson and State of Victoria Indigenous Land Use Agreement (ILUA)"@en ;
    schema:spatialCoverage <https://data.idnau.org/pid/nntt/VI2016-001> ;
    schema:startDate "2016-08-12"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """The agreement area covers about 908.5 square metres and is located approximately 3.9 kilometres west of Valencia Creek next to the Avon River. Schedule 1 describes the areas as Crown allotment 2004, 'Parish of Koorool', being the whole of the land as described in title plan TP955072J. See the attached schedule 2 for a map of the agreement area.

Commencement:

The ILUA commenced operating on the date it was signed by all parties.

Native Title Provisions:

The parties agree that the right to negotiation provisions of the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth) do not apply.

The parties agree that the surrender of native title rights and interests is intended to permanently extinguish those native title rights and interests. As such, the non-extinguishment principle does not apply.

The ILUA does have statements that reference subsection 24EB(1) or 24EBA(1) or (4) of the Native Title Act 1993(Cth). This means, that the parties consent to the State granting a fee simple title in respect of the ILUA area to the Gunaikurnai Land and Waters Aboriginal Corporation. The Gunaikurnai Land and Waters Aboriginal Corporation, acting on behalf of the Gunaikurnai people, subsequently surrendered their native title rights and interests in the area on the 12 August 2016.

Native Title in the ILUA Area:

This agreement is located within the native title determination area of the Federal Court of Australia ('FCA') proceeding Mullet on behalf of the Gunai/Kurnai People v State ofVictoria [2010] FCA 1144. (FCA File No.: VID6007/1998, NNTT File No.: VC97/4)."""@en ;
    schema:url "http://www.nntt.gov.au/searchRegApps/NativeTitleRegisters/Pages/ILUA_details.aspx?NNTT_Fileno=VI2016/001"^^xsd:anyURI .

idn-resource:9de9af26-560c-5404-8026-cec2ebbc56ea a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:references idn-resource:7b928a0d-47c5-537a-9136-066ae7f542f4 ;
    atns:category atns-cat:13e77fec-44fd-554f-a075-ec60788eb1b1 ;
    atns:country atns-country:244f6961-729f-5b21-8fc9-baff9dd9af7e ;
    atns:deleted false ;
    atns:eid "A000705"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:sourceEntityId "1182"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:09f0a068-ce65-5c11-8dec-f081d1a81ec2 ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description """The Porumalgal and Ergon Energy Electricity Indigenous Land Use Agreement (the ILUA) sets out parameters for operation and maintenance of existing electricity infrastructure in the area of the Poruma People's native title determination. The ILUA also sets out a process for validation of future works. The agreement is made between electricity providers Ergon Energy Corporation and the Poruma People native title holders, represented by their native title body corporate (NTBC) Porumalgal (Torres Strait Islander) Corporation. A determination that native title exists in the area of the Poruma People native title claim (National Native Title Tribunal file no.: QC96/81) was made in July 2000.

The conditions for use of existing infrastructure is dealt with in two categories: Ground Based Electricity Infrastructure (GBEI) and Overhead Electricity Infrastructure (OEI). The NTBC will grant a licence to Ergon to use land adjacent to GBEI that is needed to operate it and will not grant any other licence inconsistent with that. The NTBC consents to all existing OEI remaining on the determination area and will grant a lease to Ergon to access the OEI to maintain and operate it. It will not grant any leases inconsistent with that.

Under the agreement, Ergon have unrestricted access to their infrastructure, on the condition that Ergon endeavours to take the most direct route of access so as to minimise 'interference' to the NTBC and to do so in a 'culturally appropriate' way.

The ILUA confirms that any future infrastructure falls within the definition of a 'future act' under the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth) (the NTA), which means the acts are invalid. The NTBC consents to these future acts and the ILUA sets out process to validate the acts, as follows:
-Ergon will provide a 'written description of the location, nature and extent of the future works and, if necessary, a plan for the future works' to notify the NTBC;
-The NTBC is able to comment on the proposal within two months;
-Ergon is to consult with NTBC if requested;
-Ergon agrees to 'seek to modify its plans so as to eliminate or minimise the impact of the future works on native title' if the consultation process establishes that native title rights and interests would be 'significantly affected by the proposed future works.' The ILUA also specifies that compensation for these future construction activities will dealt with by the provisions of section 24KA of the NTA - this section relates to facilities for services to the public.

For future works that can not be validated through this process, the parties will negotiate an ILUA for the specific matter. Ergon may use any other means of validation under the NTA if ILUA negotiations appear unlikely to succeed after two months."""@en ;
