    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:caa3eeb7-4d71-505c-9ee9-19b8c5338435 ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description """Between:

Bronwyn De Satge, Roderick Tobane, Belinda Barrowcliffe, Shirley Blake, Gemma Cronin, Sandra Page, Lurline Lillian Burke, Cepha Roma and Brett Nutley on behalf of the Butchulla People #2 (Applicant)

And

State of Queensland, Fraser Coast Regional Council, Telstra Corporation Limited and Kingfisher Bay Resort Village Pty Ltd (Respondents)

Judge: Justice Collier

Determination

Native title exists in parts of the determination area

It consists of non-exclusive native title rights and interests over the area described in Schedule 1. For the areas over which native title does not exist, see Schedule 2.

Native title holders

Native title in the determination area is held by the Butchulla People, as described in Schedule 3.

Non-exclusive native title rights and interests over parts of the determination area

These rights exist over the area described in Schedule 1 and includes the right to:

• access, be present on, move about on and travel over the area;

• camp, and live temporarily on the area as part of camping, and for that purpose build temporary shelters;

• hunt, fish and gather on the land and waters of the area for personal, domestic and non-commercial communal purposes;

• take, use, share and exchange natural resources from the land and waters of the area for personal, domestic, and non-commercial communal purposes;

• take and use the water of the area for personal, domestic and non-commercial communal purposes;

• conduct and participate in rituals and ceremonies in the area;

• be buried on and bury native title holders within the area;

• maintain places of importance and areas of significance to the native title holders under their traditional laws and customs and protect those places and areas from physical harm;

• teach on the area the physical, cultural and spiritual attributes of the area;

• hold meetings on the area; and

• light fires on the area for personal and domestic purposes including cooking, but not for the purpose of hunting or clearing vegetation.

Other rights and interests in the determination area include:

• the rights and interests of the parties under the Fraser Island, Great Sandy National Park/ K'Gari indigenous land use agreement;

• the rights and interests of Telstra Corporation Limited;

• the rights and interests of the Fraser Coast Regional Council or any employees/agents/contactors. Such rights include those granted by legislation, those required for public access of the area, those arising out of an interest in land, and to enter the land for improvements, repairs and operational activities;

• the rights and interests of the Kingfisher Bay Resort Village Pty Ltd;

• the rights and interests of the State of Queensland as arising under law;

• the rights and interests that members of the public have, including the right to access particular features of the land; and

• any other rights or interests held by the Queensland or Commonwealth Government as defined by law.

See Schedule 4 for further information about the non-native title rights and interests.

In the case of conflict, the exercise of non-native title rights and interests will prevail over the non-exclusive native title

The Butchulla Aboriginal Corporation Registered Native Title Body Corporate (RNTBC) is the prescribed body corporate. It performs the functions required under section 57 the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth) and acts as an agent for the native title holders. The native title is not held in trust for the native title holders.

Full text of the determination is available via the URL link above."""@en ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:c74e76ef-701f-5784-8223-5fcd87a7641c,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=7908> ;
    schema:name "De Satge on behalf of the Butchulla People #2 v State of Queensland [2014] FCA 1132 TO DELETE"@en ;
    schema:startDate "2014-10-24"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """Background

Parts of the determination area were subject to military orders during World War Two. In the case of Congo and Others on behalf of the Bar-Barrum People #4 v State of Queensland [2014] FCAFC 9 the Court found that native title rights and interests can still exist whilst the order exists in the area [12]. Whilst at the time of the judgment such a decision had been appealed to the High Court, the parties agreed that the relevant areas would be included in the parts of the determination area in which native title was found to exist, subject to the capacity for the Queensland Government to seek an order to vary the determination area if the High Court found differently in the appeal of Congo [12]. Justice Collier found this to be a "practical and appropriate solution to a potentially difficult obstacle hindering a determination" of the consent determination [13].

Details of Judgment

In relation to the Butchulla People's connection to Country, anthropologist Dr Sackett stated that "there was strong evidence to support the belief of the Butchulla People that they had an obligation to care for their Country, and in particular that there would be repercussions if the Country was not cared for" [6]. As one claimant Ms Lillian/Lurline Burke said, part of being Butchulla was "Respect for country. Care for Country" [6].

The anthropologist Dr Sackett "referred in his report to the importance to the Butchulla People of passing knowledge down to succeeding generations and the associated right of Butchulla People to teach others about Butchulla Country. He noted that this tradition was being carried on, not only orally (with stories being recorded on computer) but in published form" [8].

Dr Sackett also acknowledged the barriers to maintaining a physical connection to Country, in light of colonial acts of violence and the removal of Indigenous Australians from Country [51]. Despite this, Dr Sackett was satisfied that "fundamental laws and customs continued to be acknowledged by Butchulla claimants, which meant that crucial aspects of Butchulla society were transmitted from generation to generation" [53]."""@en ;
    schema:url "http://www8.austlii.edu.au/cgi-bin/viewdoc/au/cases/cth/FCA/2014/1132.html"^^xsd:anyURI .

