        atns-sub:bda82010-54a4-5bac-8bf5-4b5341822974,
        atns-sub:c74e76ef-701f-5784-8223-5fcd87a7641c,
        atns-sub:dbff1607-a2a3-5baf-995d-8c9bb0552665,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=5625> ;
    schema:name "Doyle on behalf of the  Kalkadoon People #4  v State of Queensland (No 3) [2011] FCA 1466"@en ;
    schema:startDate "2011-12-12"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """Details of Judgement

The parties to the determination, except for Gregory Lloyd Phillips, reached an agreement as to the terms of a determination of native title to be made in relation to the Determination Area. The signed agreement was then filed on the 8 October 2010 with the Federal Court. The Court determined that it was appropriate to recognise the native title rights and interests of the Kalkadoon People in consideration of the circumstances of the agreement.

Background

The Kalkadoon people first lodged a native title application in 1995 followed by five other applications. Kalkadoon #4 was filed in 2005 with Kalkadoon #5 application lodged in 2006 and both were accepted for registration pursuant to the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth). In 2010 the court combined the #4 and #5 applications and the application was accepted for registration pursuant to s109 of the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth).

In total, the claim area covers some 38,720 square kilometres including low lying plains in the north, north east and southern sections, the Cloncurry Ranges in the mountainous centre, the City of Mt Isa, the townships of Gunpowder, Kajabbi, Quamby, Mary Kathleen and Duchess, branches and tributaries of the Leichhardt River, the south western headwaters of the Cloncurry River, and the middle and upper reaches and tributaries of the Georgina River in Central and North Queensland.

Around 4078 square kilometres will be held exclusively by the Kalkadoon people with non-exclusive native title rights held by the Kalkadoon People over the remainder of the land (National Native Title Tribunal, 2011).

Kevin Smith of Queensland South Native Title Services, stated that the determination is the end of the 18-year fight for native title recognition (ABC Rural, 2011).

Doug Bruce, one of the Kalkadoon applicants said ï¿½itï¿½s been a long hard struggle... and this is finally where we wanted to be.ï¿½ (Koori Mail, 2012).

National Native Title Tribunal President Graeme Neate congratulated all the negotiation parties on the outcome stating that is was a very significant event to achieve the consent of 42 respondents over an area of intensive mining and pastoral activity (National Native Title Tribunal, 2011). The determination was also welcomed by the then Attorney-General Robert McLelland (McLelland, 2011).

The Kalkadoon people have also signed a partnership agreement with Minerals and Metals Group (MMG) regarding the Dugald River Project, a large undeveloped zinc deposit (Koori Mail, 2012)."""@en ;
    schema:url "http://www.austlii.edu.au/cgi-bin/sinodisp/au/cases/cth/FCA/2011/1466.html?stem=0&synonyms=0&query=Kalkadoon%20People%204"^^xsd:anyURI .

