idn-resource:c18bcc8d-1ead-5fd5-8a78-33c0c97e7f81 a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    atns:category atns-cat:13e77fec-44fd-554f-a075-ec60788eb1b1 ;
    atns:deleted false ;
    atns:eid "A004259"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:sourceEntityId "4259"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:6ffe90c2-95c9-51b5-a083-8c7d83b975ef ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description """The Framework Agreement between the Mulga Mallee Regional Council, the Department of Indigenous Affairs and the City of Kalgoorlie-Boulder ('the Agreement') was signed by Brian Champion, Chairperson of the Mulga Mallee Regional Council, the Hon John Kobelke, Minister for Indigenous Affairs (Western Australia) and Ron Yuryevich, Mayor of the City of Kalgoorlie-Boulder, on 20 January 2004.

The purpose of the Agreement is 'to address the needs of Aboriginal people in Kalgoorlie-Boulder and agree to work together to establish a framework to achieve sustainable social, cultural, economic and environmental development for Aboriginal people in [the City of Kalgoorlie-Boulder]' (Agreement 2001)."""@en ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:f373baa1-6611-5aae-a140-c2cd1c548f13,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=4259> ;
    schema:name "Framework Agreement between the Mulga Mallee Regional Council, the Department of Indigenous Affairs and the City of Kalgoorlie-Boulder"@en ;
    schema:startDate "2004-01-20"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """Principles, Outcomes and Ways of Doing Business

The Agreement sets out a series of principles relating to reconciliation, respect for Aboriginal culture, promotion of equitable service delivery and openness to new approaches in funding and service delivery.

The parties agree to work collaboratively in order to:
• 'Facilitate and coordinate information exchange;
• Develop joint planning processes for the provision of Local Government services to Aboriginal people;
• Consult and negotiate on issues affecting Aboriginal people in our City; and
• Achieve practical reconciliation and resolution of issues.'

The processes that the parties will follow are developing and monitoring biennial action plans, coordinating an annual meeting of representatives of the Mulga Mallee Regional COuncil and the City of Kalgoorlie-Boulder Council and quarterly meetings involving all the parties.

Context

The Agreement was set within the framework of the Statement of Commitment to a New and Just Relationship between the Government of Western Australia and Aboriginal Western Australians, signed in 2001.

In his speech at the signing of the Agreement, the Hon John Kobelke stated that the Agreement 'is a symbol of the passion shared by the signatories for the people of Kalgoorlie-Boulder to live harmoniously and safely.' The Agreement was part of the strategies used to overcome racism in the area, following a recommendation by Dr William Jonas, acting Race Discrimination Commissioner for the Human Rights and Equal Opportunity Commission (HREOC), that the City of Kalgoorlie-Boulder appoint a mediator to address racial discrimination (Kobelke 2004).

The Minister for Indigenous Affairs stated that '[s]trategies for change ... include mediation, the launch of Kalgoorlie-Boulder’s Living in Harmony Project and action plans made after assessing three reports – a mapping and gap analysis, a needs analysis and community perception survey, and a youth needs analysis' (Government of Western Australia Department of Premier and Cabinet 2004)."""@en ;
    schema:url "http://www.dia.wa.gov.au/Publications/Files/KalBoulder%20IndigFrameAgmt.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI .

idn-resource:c1953534-2c56-506d-8d5f-7d70107c75fb a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    atns:category atns-cat:13e77fec-44fd-554f-a075-ec60788eb1b1 ;
    atns:deleted false ;
    atns:eid "A007992"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:sourceEntityId "7992"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:009cc2ae-06a8-5cd7-922a-c90a2c0cd3b2,
        atns-subcat:09f0a068-ce65-5c11-8dec-f081d1a81ec2 ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description """The Barkandji Interim Licences Indigenous Land Use Agreement ('ILUA') was agreed, under the provisions of the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth), between the Minister administering the Crown Lands Act and Barkandji Native Title Group Aboriginal Corporation (RNTBC).

The agreement area covers about 712 sq km in Western NSW extending from the NSW/SA state border in the west to Ivanhoe in the east and from the NSW/VIC state border in the south to 75km south from the NSW/QLD border.

The purpose of the agreement is to make provisions for the use of land that has previously been subject to a licence granted by the Crown Lands Minister, where the licence has been terminated and has not yet been reissued, for the five year period following the registration of the agreement.

The Native Title Representative Body for this area is NTSCORP Limited."""@en ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:c74e76ef-701f-5784-8223-5fcd87a7641c,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=7992> ;
    schema:name "Barkandji Interim Licences Indigenous Land Use Agreement (ILUA)"@en ;
    schema:spatialCoverage <https://data.idnau.org/pid/nntt/NI2018-007> ;
    schema:startDate "2018-11-20"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """Agreement area:

The agreement area covers about 712 sq km in Western NSW extending from the NSW/SA state border in the west to Ivanhoe in the east and from the NSW/Vic state border in the south to 75km south from the NSW/QLD border. The Extract from the Register of Indigenous Land Use Agreements identifies the area subject to this ILUA, per Schedule A, attachment A2 of the agreement, as all the lands and waters within areas where native title has been determined to exist within the Barkandji Traditional Owners #8 consent determination area.

Termination:

This ILUA ceases on the 20 November 2023.

Native Title Provisions:

Future act provisions
The parties consent to the Crown Land Minister re-issuing the remaining terminated licences to the former licensees for a period of up to five years from the date the agreement is entered onto the Register of Indigenous Land Use Agreements.

The parties also consent to the former licensee using and occupying the land from the date this agreement is registered to the date that the terminated licence is validly re-issued under the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth) or under this agreement. The former licensee cannot continue to use and occupy the land beyond five years from the date that this agreement is registered without having the licence re-issued.

The parties agree to the validation of the former licensee's use or occupation of the terminated licence land that occurred prior to the date this agreement was registered.Â

Where the procedures set out in Schedule C of the agreement are complied with, the parties consent to the doing of future acts that are covered by the Alternative Future Acts Regime (AFAR) as set out in Schedule C of the agreement. (Schedule C is not included in the NNTT extract). The AFAR provisions may not operate beyond five years after the registration date of the agreement.

Native Title in the Agreement Area:

This agreement is located within the native title determination area of the Federal Court of Australia (FCA) proceeding Barkandji Traditional Owners #8 (Part A) v Attorney-General of New South Wales [2015] FCA 604. (FCA file no.: NSD6084/1998, NNTT file no.: NCD2015/001)."""@en ;
    schema:url "http://www.nntt.gov.au/searchRegApps/NativeTitleRegisters/Pages/ILUA_details.aspx?NNTT_Fileno=NI2018/007"^^xsd:anyURI .

