idn-resource:e366f4be-8aa3-571d-a3ae-fd7c5c7d2abd a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    atns:category atns-cat:13e77fec-44fd-554f-a075-ec60788eb1b1 ;
    atns:country atns-country:244f6961-729f-5b21-8fc9-baff9dd9af7e ;
    atns:deleted false ;
    atns:eid "A002629"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:sourceEntityId "3019"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:1682bda6-86ad-57ab-b605-73fb7027939d ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description """The Ngumpan 'Recreational Facilities' Shared Responsibility Agreement (SRA) was signed in June 2005. The SRA focuses on providing activities for young people by upgrading sporting and recreational facilities in Ngumpan.

Shared Responsibility Agreements are agreements between governments and Indigenous communities to provide discretionary funding in return for community obligations. The new arrangements developed from an initiative of the Council of Australian Governments and replace the previous ATSIC system of funding. For more information see ‘Shared Responsibility Agreement’ below."""@en ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:5996de0c-8cc6-5f14-8901-d839af36a4d4,
        atns-sub:ef36130b-f525-5200-b398-93681fdc865b,
        atns-sub:ffe61b3b-8b29-5264-89ac-758a03928ea5,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:location "Ngumpan, Western Australia, Australia"@en ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=3019> ;
    schema:name "Ngumpan 'Recreational Facilities' Shared Responsibility Agreement (SRA)"@en ;
    schema:startDate "2005-01-01"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """Purpose

The SRA will provide funds to redevelop the existing basketball court and construct shade areas. This will allow the community at Ngumpan to participate in sporting activities despite the hot conditions there. The redeveloped basketball court will also act as a venue for meetings and cultural events.

Shared Responsibilities

Under the SRA, the community has agreed to volunteer their labour to redevelop the basketball court. The community will also run competitions, coach and referee games and encourage children to participate.

The Commonwealth Government will provide $140,000 towards the basketball court redevelopment and the construction of shade areas.

The Ngumpan SRA is administered by the Derby Indigenous Coordination Centre."""@en ;
    schema:url "http://www.indigenous.gov.au/sra/search/document.aspx?id=8170"^^xsd:anyURI .

idn-resource:e38def42-13de-5a68-8cfb-50466c3165cb a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:references idn-resource:5fa31ae7-8388-5c09-b4d1-7fb8206a9eea ;
    atns:category atns-cat:13e77fec-44fd-554f-a075-ec60788eb1b1 ;
    atns:country atns-country:244f6961-729f-5b21-8fc9-baff9dd9af7e ;
    atns:deleted false ;
    atns:eid "A000396"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:sourceEntityId "807"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:177cef4d-1963-57e3-9b5c-508480daf336,
        atns-subcat:6ffe90c2-95c9-51b5-a083-8c7d83b975ef ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description """The Ma:Mu Canopy Walk Heads of Agreement was signed by all the Mamu native title applicants, North Queensland Land Council, Wet Tropics Management Authority, Queensland Parks & Wildlife Service, and Johnstone Shire Council. The local Member Hon Warren Pitt signed as a witness on behalf of the State Government.

The agreement looks at the proposal to develop a Canopy Walk and associated visitor facilities, and also looks at gaining an undertaking from the various groups to work through concerns of the Mamu people about management of their traditional country for tourism purposes now and in the future."""@en ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:17d993e0-d19e-5349-a48e-4de57b1dba59,
        atns-sub:51ca8813-b7bb-5a37-819e-afae35771fff,
        atns-sub:7cc37d9a-fe3c-5fec-b115-d1bb8d72db9c,
        atns-sub:8ba4812a-c06b-587d-b491-67362ac3f001,
        atns-sub:e52a2091-dcda-569c-aafb-6497428c615c,
        atns-sub:f630e08a-ea9c-57e3-ab87-066e4b9ad57e,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:location "Wooroonooran National Park, Innisfail, Queensland, Australia"@en ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=807> ;
    schema:name "MaMu Canopy Walk Heads of Agreement"@en ;
    schema:startDate "2002-03-24"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """The Mamu People are registered native title claimants in Queensland (Federal Court File No. QUD6014/01). The agreement acknowledges and protects the cultural heritage values of the Mamu native title claimants. The Johnstone Shire Council and Mamu people agree to negotiate in good faith their respective interests within that native title claim and the Johnstone Shire Council agrees to be guided by the State Governmentï¿½s acceptance, for the purposes of mediation, of the Mamu as native title holders of the land and waters included in their claim for native title.

The Mamu Aboriginal Corporation will be included in any Trust, Body or Corporate entity that is established for the effective ownership of the Canopy Walk and associated freehold land and the parties agree to explore options to ensure the flow on of sustainable economic benefits to the Mamu people.

The agreement acknowledges that both the Queensland Parks and Wildlife Service and the Johnstone Shire Council have significant responsibilities for and interests in the management of the National Park and the surrounding shire.

All parties are committed to work together in good faith to develop a participatory management regime for the ecologically, economically, socially and culturally sustainable management of the National Park and surrounding tenures and to develop harmonious relationships amongst all interests in the area that recognise the unique Mamu relationship to their traditional country.

The agreement commits the parties to negotiate an Indigenous Land Use Agreement, or equivalent, to protect the native title rights and cultural heritage interests of the Mamu people in the National Park. The parties agree that areas of high conservation and cultural value shall be identified by a regional assessment process according to agreed national and international criteria. The agreement provides for an independent review acceptable to all parties in the case of a dispute in relation to the assessment of these values."""@en ;
    schema:url "http://www.aiatsis.gov.au/rsrch/ntru/ntru_hm.htm"^^xsd:anyURI .

