idn-resource:f25208a4-1044-5769-9f66-a67b1517a9b9 a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    atns:category atns-cat:13e77fec-44fd-554f-a075-ec60788eb1b1 ;
    atns:deleted false ;
    atns:eid "A006540"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:sourceEntityId "6540"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:09f0a068-ce65-5c11-8dec-f081d1a81ec2 ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description """The Anketell Port, Infrastructure Corridor and Industrial Estates Indigenous Land Use Agreement (ILUA) was agreed between:

- The State of Western Australia acting through the Premier of the State of Western Australia;

- Western Australian Land Authority; and

- Ngarluma Aboriginal Corporation RNTBC

on 23 July 2014.

The purpose of the body corporate ILUA is to grant consent by the Ngarluma Aboriginal Corporation and the Ngarluma People to the Project. The Project includes the doing of acts, including future acts, within the Agreement Area for the purposes of constructing port facilities."""@en ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:9335b2f1-4915-55cc-9736-2ded7c4a2008,
        atns-sub:d15dd1de-4696-50c8-9f10-2e95eea88e6f,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=6540> ;
    schema:name "Anketell Port, Infrastruture Corridor and Industrial Estates Indigneous Land Use Agreement (ILUA)"@en ;
    schema:spatialCoverage <https://data.idnau.org/pid/nntt/WI2014-004> ;
    schema:startDate "2014-07-23"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """Details of the Agreement

The ILUA was registered with the National Native Title Tribunal (NNTT) on 23 July 2014.

The Extract does not specify specific start or end dates for the Agreement.

However, the Extract includes reference to Clause 3.1 of the Agreement. This Clause concerns commencement of the Agreement and provides that (with the exception of Parts 2 - 5), the Agreement commences on the Execution Date. Parts 2 - 5 of the Agreement commence on the Registration Date, being 23 July 2014.

In addition, the Extract notes Clause 3.2 of the Agreement, which concerns termination. The Agreement may be terminated upon the following grounds:

- Where the State, prior to the Registration Date gives written notice to the Ngarluma Aboriginal Corporation;

- Where the State, at least six months after the Execution Date and before Registration, gives written notice to the Ngarluma Aboriginal Corporation;

- Where the State or Ngarluma Aboriginal Corporation gives a Termination Notice; or

- by mutual agreement in writing by the State and Ngarluma Aboriginal Corporation.

Native Title Provisions

Upon registration of the Agreement, Part 2 Division 3 Subdivision P of the Native Title Act is not intended to apply to:

- the granting of project titles;
- the granting of project approvals; nor
- the conduct of Project Activities

within the Non-Extinguishment Area.

The consent provided in the Agreement does constitute consent to the doing of future acts within the Non-Extinguishment Area for the purposes of sections 24BB and 24EB of the Native Title Act.

Background Information

This ILUA is the subject of the Western Australian Government's development of a new major deep water port and industrial precinct at Anketell in the Pilbara.

The ILUA constitutes project terms for the construction of a multi-user deepwater port and strategic industrial area, located 30 kilometres east of Karratha and 10 kilometres from Cape Lambert.

With this project, the Western Australian Government envisages that new export opportunities will arise due to facilitating the processing of iron ore and other minerals."""@en ;
    schema:url "http://www.nntt.gov.au/searchRegApps/NativeTitleRegisters/Pages/ILUA_details.aspx?NNTT_Fileno=WI2014/004"^^xsd:anyURI .

idn-resource:f2536bc6-5b71-581c-9ecb-2554f4f28f2e a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:references idn-resource:7b928a0d-47c5-537a-9136-066ae7f542f4 ;
    atns:category atns-cat:13e77fec-44fd-554f-a075-ec60788eb1b1 ;
    atns:country atns-country:244f6961-729f-5b21-8fc9-baff9dd9af7e ;
    atns:deleted false ;
    atns:eid "A002658"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:sourceEntityId "3048"^^xsd:token ;
    atns:subcategory atns-subcat:09f0a068-ce65-5c11-8dec-f081d1a81ec2 ;
    schema:additionalType cat-obj-types:Agreement ;
    schema:conditionsOfAccess <https://linked.data.gov.au/def/data-access-rights/open> ;
    schema:description "The Birrindudu Indigenous Land Use Agreement (ILUA) was signed by the Central Land Council and Otter Gold Pty Ltd (a subsidiary of Newmont Australia). The ILUA is a mining and exploration agreement covering an area of 2054 square kilometres in the central west of the Northern Territory. It is the 200th ILUA to be registered with the National Native Title Tribunal."@en ;
    schema:isPartOf idn-resource:d23405b4-fc04-47e2-9e7a-9c5735ae3780 ;
    schema:keywords atns-sub:17d993e0-d19e-5349-a48e-4de57b1dba59,
        atns-sub:29221ef9-81d0-59aa-a9c9-744b71ff8349,
        atns-sub:3302d162-15ce-5b7d-ad89-1f11c3cb6e0d,
        atns-sub:35e19906-2659-5157-8aee-b9d82eedbb1c,
        atns-sub:c74e76ef-701f-5784-8223-5fcd87a7641c,
        atns-sub:dc50c698-80b0-5ef4-bd95-928f2be8da05,
        atns-sub:e52a2091-dcda-569c-aafb-6497428c615c,
        "Demo" ;
    schema:location "South-west of Timber Creek, Northern Territory, Australia"@en ;
    schema:mainEntityOfPage <https://database.atns.net.au/agreement.asp?EntityID=3048> ;
    schema:name "Birrindudu Indigenous Land Use Agreement (ILUA)"@en ;
    schema:startDate "2005-09-30"^^xsd:date ;
    schema:text """Under the ILUA, the parties consent to the granting of exploration licenses and mineral leases according to the terms of the ILUA. Such terms include compliance by the license or leaseholders with the terms and conditions of their grant, consent to the location and development of water resources necessary for the treatment of any ore recovered from any granted mining lease, and the installation, operation and maintenance of equipment required for that purpose. The parties also agree to the construction, operation, repair and maintenance of a pipeline and associated equipment as well as access to the area for the exploration company.

In addition, the parties consent to the grant of an exploration retention license in the event that such a grant is a future act to which Division 3 of Part 2 of the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth) (NTA) applies. The parties thereby agree that the right to negotiate provisions of the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth) do not apply, as the alternative consultation provisions are to be followed instead. Without this agreement, the grant would be invalid and the right to negotiate provisions of the Native Title Act 1993 (Cth) would apply. Under this Act any activity, such as a grant of land, that may affect native title rights is defined as a ‘future act’ and must comply with the future act provisions of the Act in order to be valid.

The ILUA also sets out the parties' agreement with respect to:
- procedures to minimise the environmental impact of mining and exploration, and to promote the rehabilitation of areas subjected to such activities;
- the protection of native title rights and interests including the protection of sacred sites;
- the relationship between native title rights and interests and other interests in relation to the area; and
- compensation for future acts.

Finally, the parties acknowledge and agree that any future acts that may have occurred prior to the registration of the ILUA carried out in accordance with the agreement are valid to the extent that they affect native title in the area covered by the agreement."""@en .

idn-resource:f25ab29c-f575-58af-865b-f413e616a6a8 a atns:Entity,
        schema:CreativeWork ;
    dcterms:references idn-resource:3e4dcc8d-726f-5a51-a9c8-44d3b5d6a0ad ;
